453-### §8 Interaction & safety
454-
455-**R8.1 — Confirm destructive actions. (MUST)**
456-Irreversible or destructive operations **MUST** confirm before proceeding when stdin is a TTY, and **MUST** offer a non-interactive bypass. Prompt text **SHOULD** be written to stderr so stdout remains data-clean (R7.1). Tools **SHOULD NOT** open `/dev/tty` or another controlling terminal for surprise prompts when stdin is not a TTY; if a tool supports that behavior, it **MUST** document it and **MUST** disable it under `--no-input` and non-interactive/CI modes. The two bypass flags are distinct: **`--yes`/`-y`** answers the confirmation prompt (consent), while **`--force`/`-f`** overrides a guard condition — for example, the resource is protected, is in use, or has drifted from its expected state. `--force` **MUST NOT** imply routine consent; a non-interactive guarded destructive action may require both `--force` and `--yes`. Under `--no-input` with no required bypass flag, the command **MUST** fail rather than guess.
457-✅ `acme project delete demo --yes` skips the y/n prompt; `acme deploy delete web --force --yes` deletes even though it is still receiving traffic
458-❌ deleting without confirmation and without a bypass flag; requiring `--force` merely to skip a routine prompt
459-
460:**R8.2 — Non-interactive / CI behavior. (MUST)**
461-A tool **MUST** be fully usable without a TTY. It **MUST NOT** block on a prompt when input is unavailable; instead it uses provided flags/env or fails with a clear message. Use exit `2` when required non-auth input or consent is missing because prompting is disabled/unavailable, use exit `4` when authentication or authorization input is missing, and use exit `1` for runtime input failures such as unreadable files. `--no-input` forces this mode explicitly.
462-
463-**R8.3 — Idempotency & declarative flows. (SHOULD)**
464-Where it makes sense, mutating commands **SHOULD** be idempotent (re-running converges, not errors), and declarative inputs **SHOULD** use `apply` (R2.1). Distinguish imperative (`create`, `delete`) from declarative (`apply`) clearly.
465-✅ `acme widget apply --file manifest.toml` converges on re-run ❌ `apply` that fails because the resource already exists
466-
467-**R8.4 — Stronger confirmation for catastrophic actions. (MAY)**
468-For catastrophic or wide-blast-radius operations, a tool **MAY** require the user to type the resource's name (not just y/n) when interactive; `--yes` still supplies consent and `--force` still overrides guard conditions for automation (R8.1).
469-✅ "type the project name to confirm deletion:" ❌ deleting an entire project on a single keystroke
470-
471-**R8.5 — Interactive setup flows. (MAY / SHOULD)**
472:A CLI **MAY** provide interactive setup or wizard commands such as `init` or `auth login`. Interactive flows **SHOULD** be cancelable with Ctrl-C using the signal behavior in R9.6, **SHOULD** show what will be written before writing durable state, and **MUST** have a non-interactive path using flags, env, or config. Under `--no-input`, they **MUST** fail rather than prompt.
473-✅ `acme init --name demo --region us-east-1 --no-input` ❌ a required setup wizard with no flag-based path
474-
475-**R8.6 — Dry-run output contract. (SHOULD)**
476-When `--dry-run` is supported, output **SHOULD** clearly identify planned creates, updates, deletes, and external side effects, and **SHOULD** state whether validation was local-only or server-side. Machine-readable dry-run output **SHOULD** follow the stable machine-output rules in R7.2.
477-✅ `acme deploy create web --dry-run -o json` reports planned changes ❌ `--dry-run` prints `ok` without saying what would change
478-
479----
480-
481-### §9 Lifecycle & ecosystem
482-
483-**R9.1 — Completion, man pages & help topics. (SHOULD / MAY)**
484-A CLI **SHOULD** ship shell completion (`acme completion <shell>`) for at least bash, zsh, and fish, and **SHOULD** be able to generate man pages or equivalent reference docs. It **MAY** also provide non-command help *topics* via `acme help <topic>` (e.g., `formatting`, `environment`, `exit-codes`), gh-style.
485-
486-**R9.2 — Deprecation policy. (MUST)**
--
502:**R9.6 — Signals. (MUST)**
503:A tool **MUST** exit cleanly on `SIGINT` with code `130`, releasing resources, and **MUST** handle `SIGPIPE`/`EPIPE` gracefully — when a reader like `head` or `less` closes the pipe, exit quietly without a broken-pipe stack trace. It **MUST** also treat `SIGTERM` like `SIGINT` — clean up, flush (R7.7), and exit `143` (128+15) — so it behaves correctly under containers, orchestrators, and CI runners.
504-✅ `acme widget list | head -5` ends silently; `kill <pid>` shuts down cleanly with code `143` ❌ a Python traceback on `BrokenPipeError`; ignoring SIGTERM until killed with `SIGKILL`
505-
506-**R9.7 — Telemetry & update checks. (MUST / SHOULD)**
507-Telemetry **MAY** be on by default only when it is prominently documented and disclosed, minimizes collected data, and is easy to disable. A CLI with telemetry **MUST** document what is collected, why it is collected, whether it is anonymous/pseudonymous/identified, and all disable mechanisms. It **MUST** support `TOOL_TELEMETRY=0`/`false`, **SHOULD** honor `DO_NOT_TRACK=true`, and **SHOULD** provide a persistent config control such as `acme config set telemetry disabled`. Telemetry **MUST NOT** transmit secrets, full argv, argument values, environment values, file contents, request/response bodies, or resource payloads unless a separate explicit opt-in covers that data. Telemetry and update-availability checks **MUST NOT** run in non-interactive/CI contexts by default. Update checks, if present, **SHOULD** be non-blocking, cached, and disableable. A tool **MAY** offer self-update via `acme upgrade`.
508-
509-**R9.8 — Empty-state & first-run. (SHOULD)**
510-On first run or empty results, a tool **SHOULD** guide the next step rather than printing nothing or a bare error.
511-✅ `no projects yet — create one with 'acme project create <name>'`
512-❌ an empty line, or a stack trace, when there is simply nothing to show
513-
514-**R9.9 — Release integrity & self-update security. (MUST / SHOULD)**
515-If a CLI supports self-update, it **MUST** verify update authenticity and integrity before installing. Binary releases **SHOULD** publish checksums and signatures or equivalent provenance, and update mechanisms **SHOULD** document channels, rollback behavior, and how to disable update checks (R9.7).
516-✅ `acme upgrade` verifies a signed artifact before replacing the binary ❌ downloading and executing an unauthenticated update
517-
